{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01975V57QGnTyFsVYiAyH1Jg","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/cert_manip_resist_eval/report.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Handover — 2026-05-22\\\\n\\\\n> 由 Agent 生成，每次状态变更后自动更新。硬限 150 行。\\\\n\\\\n## 运行中\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\\n## 待处理\\\\n- plan001_step1_batched_v2（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_batched（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_v3（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_throttled_v5（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_throttled_v4（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_throttled_v3（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_throttled_v2（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_throttled（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_v2（准备中）\\\\n- plan001_step1_parallel_final（准备中）\\\\n- …另有 13 项\\\\n\\\\n## 近期结论\\\\n- **plan001_step1_full_v4** (success): Expanding from 6 to 15 models (9 API + 6 local) across 6 families and 3 capability tiers breaks the structural K_eff-η_max collinearity (r=-0.8 with 6 models). With 15 models, panels can have high K_eff (cross-family diversity) without necessarily including weak judges, enabling clean separation of diversity vs robustness effects.\\\\n- **taxonomy-broad-spectrum** (negative): K_eff is a significant negative predictor of panel ASR specifically for broad-spectrum attacks (verbosity bias, position bias, authority bias) that exploit shared judge biases, consistent across both MMLU and ARC datasets. This validates the attack taxonomy: targeted attacks → η_max dominates; broad-spectrum attacks → K_eff provides additional protection. ← 基于 mvp-step2v3-retry3\\\\n- **mvp-step2v3-retry3** (negative): Controlling for individual model robustness (η_max), higher K_eff panels have significantly lower ASR. Multi-factor regression log(ASR) ~ log(K_eff) + log(η_max) should show K_eff coefficient significantly negative. ← 基于 mvp-step2v3-retry2\\\\n\\\\n## 趋势\\\\n成功 1 / 负向 3 / 失败 3 / 共 7\\\\n\\\\n## 下一步\\\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\\\n\\\\n## 资源状态\\\\nseetacloud-rtx6000: 空闲 cuda:0, cuda:1, cuda:2, cuda:3\\\\n\\\\n## 告警\\\\n- mvp-step2v3-regression: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- mvp-step2v3-retry: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- mvp-step2v3-retry2: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 1736, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AfraqM1gscS8e9AddmhuYW","session_id":"6b6bb46a-6f14-4907-ac47-60e29122a37c","uuid":"cacc6ce2-eda1-4700-99ff-a5b5094e6ffb","timestamp":"2026-05-22T18:40:29.817Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BkcFdb8TU1JZV7okSFfYhj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Threat Model and Problem Formulation}\n2\t\n3\tA \\emph{panel} $\\mathcal{P} = \\{J_1, \\ldots, J_K\\}$ consists of $K$ judges (with $K$ odd) that evaluate a candidate text via majority vote: the text is accepted if at least $t = \\lceil K/2 \\rceil$ judges approve it. Under an attack $a$, each judge $J_j$ is compromised with marginal failure probability $\\eta_j(a) = \\Pr(X_j^a = 1)$, where $X_j^a \\in \\{0,1\\}$ denotes the failure indicator. We write $\\eta_{\\max} = \\max_j \\eta_j$ for the vulnerability of the weakest judge. The \\emph{panel attack success rate} is $\\mathrm{ASR}(\\mathcal{P}, a) = \\Pr\\!\\bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^K X_j^a \\geq t\\bigr)$.\n4\t\n5\tTo quantify diversity, we define the \\emph{effective independence} $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ directly from pairwise mutual information among judges:\n6\t\\begin{equation}\n7\t\\label{eq:keff}\n8\tK_{\\mathrm{eff}} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K} \\Bigl[1 + \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\exp\\!\\bigl(-2 \\cdot \\mathrm{MI}(i,j)\\bigr)\\Bigr].\n9\t\\end{equation}\n10\tThis formulation computes effective independence from pairwise MI estimates without matrix decomposition, and is more stable for small panels ($K{=}3$). It ranges from $K_{\\mathrm{eff}} \\approx K$ when judges are nearly independent ($\\mathrm{MI} \\to 0$) to $K_{\\mathrm{eff}} \\approx 1$ when judges are highly correlated ($\\mathrm{MI} \\to \\infty$). Under the strong data-processing inequality (SDPI), the joint vulnerability satisfies $\\eta_{\\mathrm{joint}} \\leq \\min(1,\\; K_{\\mathrm{eff}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\max})$, suggesting that diversity should help---but this bound assumes non-adaptive faults.\n11\t\n12\t\\begin{definition}[Adaptive Adversary]\n13\t\\label{def:adversary}\n14\tAn \\emph{adaptive adversary} has full knowledge of the panel composition $\\mathcal{P}$, the per-judge vulnerability profiles $\\{\\eta_j(\\cdot)\\}_{j=1}^K$, and the inter-judge correlation structure. It selects the attack $a^* = \\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}} \\mathrm{ASR}(\\mathcal{P}, a)$ to maximize the panel ASR, yielding $\\mathrm{ASR}^*(\\mathcal{P}) = \\sup_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}} \\mathrm{ASR}(\\mathcal{P}, a)$.\n15\t\\end{definition}\n16\t\n17\tThis departs from Byzantine fault-tolerance (BFT) models~\\cite{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}, which tolerate arbitrary (including malicious) faults provided the number of faulty nodes satisfies $f < n/3$, but do not model adversaries that strategically target system structure. An adaptive adversary instead concentrates attack effort on the weakest link~\\cite{schneier2000secrets}, rendering diversity-based defenses less effective than the SDPI bound implies.\n18\t\n19\tThe distinction reveals a \\emph{reversal mechanism}: under random faults, independent judges fail via uncorrelated events, and majority voting aggregates away individual errors---higher $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ reduces joint failure probability. Under adaptive adversaries, however, independent judges present a \\emph{wider attack surface}: the adversary can exploit judge-specific vulnerabilities across a broader space of attacks, because functionally diverse judges respond to different manipulation strategies. Diversity ceases to provide mutual protection and instead expands the set of viable attack vectors.\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{theorem}[Weakest-Link Monotonicity]\n22\t\\label{thm:monotonicity}\n23\tLet $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{P}'$ be two panels sharing $K{-}1$ judges and differing only in the $K$-th member, where $J_K'$ is pointwise more vulnerable than $J_K$ (i.e., $\\Pr(X_{K'}^a {=} 1 \\mid \\mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \\mathbf{x}_{-K}) \\geq \\Pr(X_K^a {=} 1 \\mid \\mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \\mathbf{x}_{-K})$ for all attacks $a$ and realizations $\\mathbf{x}_{-K}$). Then for any adversary, including an adaptive one: $\\mathrm{ASR}^*(\\mathcal{P}') \\geq \\mathrm{ASR}^*(\\mathcal{P})$.\n24\t\\end{theorem}\n25\t\n26\t\\noindent Proof in Appendix~\\ref{app:proof}. Intuitively, the replaced judge is \\emph{pivotal} exactly when $t{-}1$ of the remaining judges have already failed; in such configurations, a more vulnerable replacement can only increase the probability of tipping the majority, never decrease it. The result holds under arbitrary inter-judge correlations and any attack space $\\mathcal{A}$.\n27\t\n28\tThe theorem establishes that $\\eta_{\\max}$ is monotonically linked to panel vulnerability, but is silent on the role of diversity. To disentangle the two drivers, we fit a regression $\\log(\\mathrm{ASR}) \\sim \\beta_1 \\log(\\eta_{\\max}) + \\beta_2 \\log(K_{\\mathrm{eff}})$ across panels. The theorem predicts $\\beta_1 > 0$; the SDPI bound under non-adaptive faults would suggest $\\beta_2 \\leq 0$; but the reversal mechanism predicts $\\beta_2 > 0$ under adaptive adversaries---diversity independently increases vulnerability. Our experiments in \\S\\ref{sec:experiments} test these competing predictions across 12 conditions spanning two benchmarks and six attack types, using dominance analysis to isolate each driver's independent contribution."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012xczkK9L1sXuWP3BAWbci6","session_id":"6b6bb46a-6f14-4907-ac47-60e29122a37c","uuid":"6bd88d13-dfe1-4a41-93a3-c4aef8eba725","timestamp":"2026-05-22T18:40:29.945Z"}
